# 6. PROJECT DETAIL PAGE

Every project must have its own detailed page.

Suggested route:

`/project-tutorials/[slug]`

The page should contain:

### A. Project Overview

* Project title.
* Description.
* What the user will build.
* Why this project is useful.
* Real-world applications.
* Expected final outcome.

### B. Technical Information

* Programming language.
* Frameworks and libraries.
* Technologies.
* Tools required.
* Prerequisites.
* Difficulty level.
* Estimated duration, when available.

### C. Learning Outcomes

Explain what the user will understand after completing the project.

For example:

* Understanding HTTP request handling.
* Learning database operations.
* Understanding how an interpreter works.
* Implementing authentication.
* Learning memory management.

Learning outcomes must be relevant to the actual project.

### D. Project Resources

Provide verified references such as:

* Original tutorial.
* Relevant GitHub repository.
* Official documentation.
* Language documentation.
* Framework documentation.
* Additional learning resources.

All external references must be clickable and open correctly.

### E. Project Building Instructions

This is the most important part.

Every project should have a structured building workflow.

Where source material supports it, divide the project into meaningful stages:

1. Understand the project.
2. Understand prerequisites.
3. Set up the development environment.
4. Initialize the project.
5. Build the core functionality.
6. Implement additional features.
7. Test the implementation.
8. Debug and improve.
9. Complete and review the project.

Do not force identical steps onto every project. Each tutorial should have a workflow appropriate to its actual technical requirements.

